when you ask people what makes a great  horror film many say it's the thrill of  being scared each year horror films make  millions by incorporating quote an  overwhelming and painful feeling caused  by something frightfully shocking  terrifying or revolting in fact the  actual number of horror films produced  worldwide have increased exponentially  since the 1930s and with recent  successes such as it and get out it  looks like that trend will continue but  what separates a good horror film from a  great one is it the amount of blood your  scares of terrifying villain although  those are indeed elements to some horror  films they aren't the most important in  fact many horror films today forget the  cornerstone of great filmmaking that's a  great story but first let's briefly look  at where horror films too often go wrong  the jump scare has become a common  tactic used in many films but its  reliance for cheap thrills in modern  horror can sometimes become a hindrance  according to where's the jump com an  online database which tracks the number  of jump scares and movies the top three  films with jump scares average is  staggering 30 jumps per film that's one  jump every three minutes not  surprisingly they also averaged a dismal  4.8 on IMDB and how many times have we  seen something like this  [Music]  or something like this no no the service  your sucks  horror movies today rely far too heavily  on these cliches they're stale  uninteresting and don't bring anything  new to the genre in other words they're  derivative it's part of the reason many  claim the slasher film is dead  everything's been done to death  cliches take us out of the movie and  thus out of the poor finally many horror  films especially Hollywood horror  focused too much on gore or shock fede  alvarez --is remake of Evil Dead is a  good example story takes a backseat to  over-the-top gore at the same time  losing the lovable charm of the original  that endeared us to Bruce Campbell's ash  Williams but what I really want to get  to is an element often glossed over in  horror writing that's empathizing with  the character as I like to say scaring  is caring 20 17s get out is a great  example of empathy driven horror in it  Jordi Peale has created several  empathetic characteristics in the  character of Chris Washington now it's  important to know the difference between  empathy and sympathy because far too  many horror films make the mistake  relying too heavily on sympathy that's  thinking because something bad is  happening as someone will automatically  care about them not true when we  empathize with the character we're  relating to them we are connecting  connection equals investment  whereas sympathy is disconnection no  investment how many hundreds of horror  films have you seen where a character is  being brutally murdered and you sit back  and go damn that sucks but there's no  investment at all which leads me to a  great example of empathy driven [ __ ] 27  teens get out remember  empathy equals connection so how does he  do this first Jordan puts Chris in  situations we can all relate to and what  better situation than meeting your  partner's family for the first time  we've all been there  awkwardness the tension reckon we  connect second Chris cares about his  girlfriend he loves her and wants her  white upper-class family not to be  caught off guard when he shows up he  knows that pleasing her family will  please her third he has a bad habit that  he wants to quit you smoke Chris we've  all at one time or another wanted to  quit something whether it be drugs  alcohol or even just to lose a little  bit of weight regardless we connect  there are many other such  characteristics apply to Chris such as  his love for animals dream of becoming a  photographer but what peel does  fantastically here is link those  empathetic characteristics to the story  itself meeting the family provides the  backdrop or the context for which the  story takes place and he does that out  of love for his girlfriend and quitting  smoking becomes a dramatic tool to get  him to agree to the hypnosis this is  character driving story but the real  danger for Chris team doesn't even begin  until 65 minutes into the movie by that  time we've connected with him on such a  level that the scares and war of the  final act are greatly intensified we  care about him we want to see him  survive and this is born out of our  empathy out of our connection so the  next time you find yourself dozing off  to a horror flick ask yourself this  question am i connecting with the  character if not you may just want to  see them die for the fun of it instead  of for the thrill of it as Tom's shown  from The Guardian puts it for today's  sophisticated audience it's not all  about jump scares the best horror movies  keep playing even if you shut your eyes  hey if you like what you watch make sure  to subscribe and leave a comment below  [Music]
 

